Write code quality finding notes for session {{ session_id }}

Review the code the assistant wrote or changed in session {{ session_id }} and
look for code quality issues:

- Project rule violations
- Best practice violations (your judgement)
- Poor stack alignment (missing useful libraries, working against libraries)
- Bugs the session did not detect

The evidence must be in the record itself. Do not infer or assume conditions
beyond what the record shows. Do not report issues the session itself
diagnoses and fixes.

Many sessions contain no code quality issues. Do not force findings onto a
session.

{% if project %}
## Project context

The session belongs to project `{{ project }}`. Summary notes for the project
describe its rules and stack. Read them first via `mcp__gage__Query`:

`SELECT name, value FROM note WHERE target = 'project:{{ project }}' AND name LIKE 'project-summary.%'`

Judge the session's code against these summaries. A rule violation finding
must name the rule it violates.
{% endif %}

## Reading the session

Read the conversation by querying the `message` table via `mcp__gage__Query`,
filtering by `session_id = '{{ session_id }}'`. `message` table columns:
(session_id, line, uuid, type, subtype, text, timestamp, attachments,
ide_tags, raw). Code changes appear as Edit and Write tool calls.

## Writing findings

Write findings using the `mcp__gage__Finding` tool using a single request
rather than one tool use per finding. This saves time and tokens.

If you did not find issues for the session, do not use `mcp__gage__Finding`
to report "no issues found". Only use the tool to report found issues.

In your reply, simply state how many findings you wrote. If you didn't write
any findings, say so. Do not re-state the findings in your reply.
